{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc | sed -n '1,400p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yin.vm.debruijn\n     2\t  \"Alpha-canonical de Bruijn projection of the named Universal AST\n     3\t   (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md).\n     4\t\n     5\t   This namespace is D0-D3 of that design: the published\n     6\t   :yin.debruijn/* dimension and its hash domain, §5's canonical value\n     7\t   table with the NFC seam, §2's root framing and input validation, §3's\n     8\t   scope resolver, §4-§5's Merkle records — node hashes over the\n     9\t   dimension hash, hash-consed records with canonically spelled scalars,\n    10\t   and the root fingerprint — the settled canonical byte encoder whose\n    11\t   rules make those bytes identical on the JVM, JS, and Dart hosts, and\n    12\t   the pure d5 storage adapter over the projected records. The dao.stream\n    13\t   forward-step is D4's; it is not here.\n    14\t\n    15\t   The projection is an interpreter over plain AST datoms and never\n    16\t   executes values, primitives, streams, continuations, or effects. Its\n    17\t   input domain is a fully macro-expanded Universal AST, so an\n    18\t   unexpanded macro call site is a terminal diagnostic: an application\n    19\t   whose operator is a :lambda carrying :macro? true is unexpanded. A free\n    20\t   variable whose name matches a registry macro cannot be detected from\n    21\t   these datoms — that detection limit is inherited and documented here,\n    22\t   not enforced.\n    23\t\n    24\t   Input contract (§2): assert-only d5 datoms (only the reserved\n    25\t   :db/retract op is diagnosed; any other provenance m stays valid, and t\n    26\t   and m never enter projected identity); exactly one :yin/root true fact\n    27\t   per graph; the fact index resets at every root-frame boundary because\n    28\t   emitter tempids may restart at -16; attributes outside the :yin\n    29\t   namespace are ignored; :yin/tail? and :yin/macro-name are\n    30\t   tolerated-and-ignored emission metadata; an unknown :yin/* attribute\n    31\t   on a walked node is a diagnostic.\n    32\t\n    33\t   Scope convention (§3, the owner's settled decisions): frame depth 0 is\n    34\t   the innermost frame, position is the source parameter index with the\n    35\t   leftmost parameter at 0, frames search inner-to-outer, and positions\n    36\t   search right-to-left within a frame — duplicate parameters are\n    37\t   rightmost-wins, matching `yin.vm.engine/bind-params`. A bound\n    38\t   occurrence is {:bound [frame-depth position]}; a free one {:free name}.\n    39\t\n    40\t   Canonical limits fixed in D0 (Section 5): NFC normalisation collisions\n    41\t   are an inherited limit of the repository encoding; int64 and double\n    42\t   are disjoint classes (contract version 1), so 1 and 1.0 never merge\n    43\t   on JVM/Dart; the Dart NFC source is the unorm_dart package\n    44\t   (Unicode 16.0, owner-settled 2026-09-21) behind the one\n    45\t   host-dispatched `normalize-nfc` seam.\"\n    46\t  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n    47\t            [dao.jing :as jing]\n    48\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\n    49\t            #?(:cljd [\"package:unorm_dart/unorm_dart.dart\" :as unorm]))\n    50\t  #?(:cljd (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" ByteData Uint8List])))\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t;; =============================================================================\n    54\t;; D0: the published dimension and its hash domain (§4, datom.md §META-PROTOCOL)\n    55\t;; =============================================================================\n    56\t\n    57\t(def contract-version\n    58\t  \"The :yin.debruijn/* contract version. Version 1 is type-preserving:\n    59\t   :int64 and :double are disjoint, and integral doubles are never\n    60\t   folded into :int64.\"\n    61\t  1)\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    64\t(def canonical-value-table\n    65\t  \"§5's canonical value table as data: the classes a projected scalar may\n    66\t   carry and the rule that fixes each one's encoding. Bounds are symbolic\n    67\t   (:signed-int64, :ieee-754-safe-integer) so the table — and therefore\n    68\t   the descriptor that embeds it — prints identically on every host;\n    69\t   `canonical-class` below pins the numbers. D3's encoder consumes this\n    70\t   table; D0 consumes it for validation.\"\n    71\t  {:classes [:nil :bool :int64 :double :string :bytes :keyword :symbol\n    72\t             :map :set :vector :list],\n    73\t   :framing {:slots :tagged-and-length-delimited},\n    74\t   :nil {:encoding :own-tag},\n    75\t   :bool {:bytes {false \"00\", true \"01\"}},\n    76\t   :int64 {:domain :signed-int64, :endianness :little},\n    77\t   :double {:format :ieee-754,\n    78\t            :nan :one-quiet-nan,\n    79\t            :signed-zero :distinct,\n    80\t            :integral-double-folding false},\n    81\t   :string {:encoding :utf-8, :normalization :nfc, :framing :length-prefixed},\n    82\t   :bytes {:framing :length-prefixed},\n    83\t   :keyword {:components [:namespace :name], :encoding :nfc-utf-8},\n    84\t   :symbol {:components [:namespace :name], :encoding :nfc-utf-8},\n    85\t   :map {:key-order :canonical-encoded-bytes},\n    86\t   :set {:element-order :canonical-encoded-bytes},\n    87\t   ;; vectors and lists are distinct classes: `vector?` observes the\n    88\t   ;; difference, so one class would be an undeclared semantic collision\n    89\t   :vector {:order :positional},\n    90\t   :list {:order :positional},\n    91\t   :numbers {:javascript {:int64 :safe-integer-only,\n    92\t                          :unsafe-integer :diagnostic},\n    93\t             :out-of-domain [:bigint :ratio :char :other-numeric]},\n    94\t   :limits [:nfc-normalization-collisions]})\n    95\t\n    96\t\n    97\t(def dimension-slots\n    98\t  \"The projected d5 semantic slots in §4's declared order, each as\n    99\t   [slot-name slot-type slot-role]. Roles: :scalar enters node identity;\n   100\t   :child is an ordered child ref carrying child hashes; :marker is\n   101\t   storage/index (§5 excludes :yin.debruijn/hash and :yin.debruijn/root\n   102\t   from every node hash). :yin.debruijn/operands is the one\n   103\t   ordered-vector slot.\"\n   104\t  [[:yin.debruijn/hash :hash :marker]\n   105\t   [:yin.debruijn/type :keyword :scalar]\n   106\t   [:yin.debruijn/arity :int64 :scalar]\n   107\t   [:yin.debruijn/bound [:tuple :int64 :int64] :scalar]\n   108\t   [:yin.debruijn/free :symbol :scalar]\n   109\t   [:yin.debruijn/value :canonical :scalar]\n   110\t   [:yin.debruijn/op :keyword :scalar]\n   111\t   [:yin.debruijn/key :symbol :scalar]\n   112\t   [:yin.debruijn/prefix :string :scalar]\n   113\t   [:yin.debruijn/buffer :int64 :scalar]\n   114\t   [:yin.debruijn/parked-id :keyword :scalar]\n   115\t   [:yin.debruijn/macro? :bool :scalar]\n   116\t   [:yin.debruijn/body :ref :child]\n   117\t   [:yin.debruijn/operator :ref :child]\n   118\t   [:yin.debruijn/operands [:ordered-vector :ref] :child]\n   119\t   [:yin.debruijn/test :ref :child]\n   120\t   [:yin.debruijn/consequent :ref :child]\n   121\t   [:yin.debruijn/alternate :ref :child]\n   122\t   [:yin.debruijn/target :ref :child]\n   123\t   [:yin.debruijn/val-node :ref :child]\n   124\t   [:yin.debruijn/source :ref :child]\n   125\t   [:yin.debruijn/root :bool :marker]])\n   126\t\n   127\t\n   128\t(def descriptor\n   129\t  \"The :yin.debruijn/* dimension published per datom.md's meta-protocol:\n   130\t   the small d3 subgraph [s a v] declaring arity, ordered slots, the\n   131\t   canonical encoding rule parameterized by the slot types, and the\n   132\t   anchor morphisms. The content hash of this subgraph — `dimension-hash`\n   133\t   below — IS the dimension's identity and the projection's hash domain\n   134\t   separator: not an ad hoc text string.\"\n   135\t  [[:yin.debruijn/dimension :dim/contract-version contract-version]\n   136\t   [:yin.debruijn/dimension :dim/arity 22]\n   137\t   [:yin.debruijn/dimension :dim/slots dimension-slots]\n   138\t   [:yin.debruijn/dimension\n   139\t    :dim/encoding\n   140\t    {:hash :sha256,\n   141\t     :node-hash \"SHA-256(dimension-hash || encode(tag-specific-slots-in-descriptor-order))\",\n   142\t     :slot-order :descriptor,\n   143\t     :tag-specific-slots-only true,\n   144\t     :hash-excluded-slots [:yin.debruijn/hash :yin.debruijn/root],\n   145\t     :child-order :declared,\n   146\t     :values canonical-value-table}]\n   147\t   [:yin.debruijn/dimension\n   148\t    :dim/projection-to\n   149\t    [[:d1 \"content-addressing floor: every projected node reduces to its node hash\"]\n   150\t     [:d3 \"semantic floor: each projected record is (h, :yin.debruijn/<slot>, v)\"]]]\n   151\t   [:yin.debruijn/dimension\n   152\t    :dim/lift-from\n   153\t    [[:d5 \"the named :yin/* Universal AST datoms (yin.vm/ast->datoms-with-root)\"\n   154\t      \"yin.vm.debruijn/project-datoms is the lift\"]]]])\n   155\t\n   156\t\n   157\t;; dimension-hash is minted below, beside the canonical encoder: §5's\n   158\t;; domain separator is the descriptor encoded through the settled encoder\n   159\t;; and hashed, and the encoder's definitions intervene between the two.\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t;; =============================================================================\n   163\t;; D0: the canonical value table as validation, and the NFC seam (§5)\n   164\t;; =============================================================================\n   165\t\n   166\t(defn- bytes-like?\n   167\t  [v]\n   168\t  #?(:clj (bytes? v)\n   169\t     :cljs (instance? js/Uint8Array v)\n   170\t     :cljd (instance? Uint8List v)))\n   171\t\n   172\t\n   173\t#?(:cljs\n   174\t   (defn- js-number-class\n   175\t     \"The JS adapter's classification of a JS number, whose one type cannot\n   176\t      tell 1 from 1.0 (a host limit that stays here, never in the universal\n   177\t      projection): a safe integer is :int64; NaN, the infinities, -0.0, and\n   178\t      non-integral values are :double; an unsafe integral is nil, a\n   179\t      diagnostic rather than a silently misclaimed int64.\"\n   180\t     [v]\n   181\t     (cond\n   182\t       (not= v v) :double\n   183\t       (or (= js/Infinity v) (= js/-Infinity v)) :double\n   184\t       (and (zero? v) (= js/-Infinity (/ 1.0 v))) :double\n   185\t       (not= v (js/Math.floor v)) :double\n   186\t       (<= -9007199254740991 v 9007199254740991) :int64\n   187\t       :else nil)))\n   188\t\n   189\t\n   190\t(defn- numeric-class\n   191\t  \"Classify a number under the type-preserving numeric rule (contract\n   192\t   version 1): int64 and double are disjoint. Host integers (Long on the\n   193\t   JVM, Dart int) are :int64 and every floating-point value is :double,\n   194\t   integral or not; bigints, ratios, and friends classify nil. Only the\n   195\t   :cljs adapter, where 1 and 1.0 are one number, classifies by value.\"\n   196\t  [v]\n   197\t  #?(:clj (cond\n   198\t            (int? v) :int64\n   199\t            (float? v) :double\n   200\t            :else nil)\n   201\t     :cljs (js-number-class v)\n   202\t     :cljd (if (int? v) :int64 :double)))\n   203\t\n   204\t\n   205\t(defn- code-unit-at\n   206\t  [s i]\n   207\t  #?(:clj (int (.charAt ^String s i))\n   208\t     :cljs (.charCodeAt s i)\n   209\t     :cljd (.codeUnitAt s i)))\n   210\t\n   211\t\n   212\t(defn- well-formed-utf16?\n   213\t  \"True when every surrogate in `s` is paired — the precondition for the\n   214\t   hosts' UTF-8 encoders to agree. An unpaired surrogate reaches storage\n   215\t   as itself on some hosts and as the replacement character on others,\n   216\t   so byte identity is unmakeable there: such a string classifies\n   217\t   unsupported, in a value or in an ident's parts alike.\"\n   218\t  [s]\n   219\t  (let [n (count s)]\n   220\t    (loop [i 0]\n   221\t      (if (>= i n)\n   222\t        true\n   223\t        (let [c (code-unit-at s i)]\n   224\t          (cond\n   225\t            (<= 0xDC00 c 0xDFFF) false\n   226\t            (<= 0xD800 c 0xDBFF) (if (and (< (inc i) n)\n   227\t                                          (<= 0xDC00 (code-unit-at s (inc i)) 0xDFFF))\n   228\t                                   (recur (+ i 2))\n   229\t                                   false)\n   230\t            :else (recur (inc i))))))))\n   231\t\n   232\t\n   233\t(defn- ident-parts-ok?\n   234\t  \"An identifier classifies only when its namespace and name are both\n   235\t   well-formed UTF-16: ident parts are strings under NFC.\"\n   236\t  [x]\n   237\t  (and (or (nil? (namespace x))\n   238\t           (well-formed-utf16? (namespace x)))\n   239\t       (well-formed-utf16? (name x))))\n   240\t\n   241\t\n   242\t(defn canonical-class\n   243\t  \"Classify `v` under §5's canonical value table: the class keyword when\n   244\t   v is inside the canonical domain, nil when v is unsupported (a\n   245\t   diagnostic at the walk). Recursive over collections; maps and sets\n   246\t   sort by canonical encoded bytes only at D3 encode time, so any\n   247\t   iteration order classifies identically. Records are not the plain\n   248\t   map they print as — their host shapes disagree — so they diagnose.\"\n   249\t  [v]\n   250\t  (cond\n   251\t    (nil? v) :nil\n   252\t    (boolean? v) :bool\n   253\t    (string? v) (when (well-formed-utf16? v) :string)\n   254\t    (keyword? v) (when (ident-parts-ok? v) :keyword)\n   255\t    (symbol? v) (when (ident-parts-ok? v) :symbol)\n   256\t    (bytes-like? v) :bytes\n   257\t    (map? v) (when (and (not (record? v))\n   258\t                        (every? (fn [entry]\n   259\t                                  (and (canonical-class (key entry))\n   260\t                                       (canonical-class (val entry))))\n   261\t                                v))\n   262\t               :map)\n   263\t    (set? v) (when (every? canonical-class v) :set)\n   264\t    (vector? v) (when (every? canonical-class v) :vector)\n   265\t    (sequential? v) (when (every? canonical-class v) :list)\n   266\t    (number? v) (numeric-class v)))\n   267\t\n   268\t\n   269\t(defn canonical-value?\n   270\t  \"True when `v` is inside §5's canonical value domain — equivalently,\n   271\t   when `canonical-class` is non-nil.\"\n   272\t  [v]\n   273\t  (some? (canonical-class v)))\n   274\t\n   275\t\n   276\t(defn normalize-nfc\n   277\t  \"The one NFC seam of the projection (§5): every string entering\n   278\t   canonical encoding passes through here. :clj delegates to\n   279\t   java.text.Normalizer, :cljs to String.prototype.normalize, and :cljd\n   280\t   to the unorm_dart package (Unicode 16.0, owner-settled 2026-09-21) so\n   281\t   every ClojureDart target normalizes identically. NFC normalisation\n   282\t   collisions — distinct inputs with one composed form — are an\n   283\t   inherited limit of the repository encoding.\"\n   284\t  [^String s]\n   285\t  #?(:clj (java.text.Normalizer/normalize s java.text.Normalizer$Form/NFC)\n   286\t     :cljs (.normalize s \"NFC\")\n   287\t     :cljd (unorm/nfc s)))\n   288\t\n   289\t\n   290\t;; =============================================================================\n   291\t;; D0: the walked node grammar (§2)\n   292\t;; =============================================================================\n   293\t\n   294\t(def node-grammar\n   295\t  \"§2's walked node grammar and child order as the walk vocabulary —\n   296\t   exactly the emitter's table. Scalars are [attribute slot-kind] in\n   297\t   declared order; children are [attribute cardinality] in the fixed\n   298\t   walk order. Slot kinds: :canonical is the full §5 value domain;\n   299\t   :syms is an ordered vector of symbols; the rest are the obvious\n   300\t   scalar types.\"\n   301\t  {:literal {:scalars [[:yin/value :canonical]], :children []}\n   302\t   :variable {:scalars [[:yin/name :symbol]], :children []}\n   303\t   :lambda {:scalars [[:yin/params :syms] [:yin/macro? :bool]],\n   304\t            :children [[:yin/body :node]]}\n   305\t   :application {:scalars [],\n   306\t                 :children [[:yin/operator :node] [:yin/operands :nodes]]}\n   307\t   :dao.stream.apply/call {:scalars [[:yin/op :keyword]],\n   308\t                           :children [[:yin/operands :nodes]]}\n   309\t   :if {:scalars [],\n   310\t        :children [[:yin/test :node] [:yin/consequent :node]\n   311\t                   [:yin/alternate :node]]}\n   312\t   :vm/gensym {:scalars [[:yin/prefix :string]], :children []}\n   313\t   :vm/store-get {:scalars [[:yin/key :symbol]], :children []}\n   314\t   :vm/store-put {:scalars [[:yin/key :symbol] [:yin/value :canonical]],\n   315\t                  :children []}\n   316\t   :vm/current-continuation {:scalars [], :children []}\n   317\t   :vm/park {:scalars [], :children []}\n   318\t   :vm/resume {:scalars [[:yin/parked-id :keyword]],\n   319\t               :children [[:yin/val-node :node]]}\n   320\t   :stream/make {:scalars [[:yin/buffer :int64]], :children []}\n   321\t   :stream/put {:scalars [],\n   322\t                :children [[:yin/target :node] [:yin/val-node :node]]}\n   323\t   :stream/cursor {:scalars [], :children [[:yin/source :node]]}\n   324\t   :stream/next {:scalars [], :children [[:yin/source :node]]}\n   325\t   :stream/close {:scalars [], :children [[:yin/source :node]]}})\n   326\t\n   327\t\n   328\t(def ^:private optional-attributes\n   329\t  \"§2's optional scalar: only :yin/macro? may be absent from a\n   330\t   well-formed emitter batch. Every other grammar slot is required.\"\n   331\t  #{:yin/macro?})\n   332\t\n   333\t\n   334\t(def ^:private tolerated-attributes\n   335\t  \"The :yin/* facts legal on a walked node of any type: :yin/type, the\n   336\t   :yin/root framing fact, and §2's tolerated-and-ignored emission\n   337\t   metadata.\"\n   338\t  #{:yin/type :yin/root :yin/tail? :yin/macro-name})\n   339\t\n   340\t\n   341\t(def ^:private known-attributes\n   342\t  \"Per node type, the complete legal :yin/* vocabulary on a walked node\n   343\t   of that type: its grammar row's slots plus the tolerated set. A known\n   344\t   attribute on a type whose row does not list it — a :yin/body on a\n   345\t   :literal, dangling or not — is as unknown there as :yin/extra, never\n   346\t   silently ignored.\"\n   347\t  (into {}\n   348\t        (map (fn [[tag {:keys [scalars children]}]]\n   349\t               [tag (into tolerated-attributes\n   350\t                          (map first)\n   351\t                          (concat scalars children))]))\n   352\t        node-grammar))\n   353\t\n   354\t\n   355\t;; =============================================================================\n   356\t;; D0: root framing and input validation (§2)\n   357\t;; =============================================================================\n   358\t\n   359\t(defn- check-datom\n   360\t  \"Validate one input datom as a d5 [e a v t m] assert. Shape first, then\n   361\t   §2's assert-only rule: m must not be dao.datom's reserved :db/retract\n   362\t   op — read through `reserved`, never a bare literal. An assert carrying\n   363\t   any other provenance m stays valid; once this passes, t and m are\n   364\t   excluded from projected identity.\"\n   365\t  [d]\n   366\t  (when-not (and (vector? d) (= 5 (count d)))\n   367\t    (throw (ex-info \"Malformed d5 datom in projection input\"\n   368\t                    {:rule :malformed-datom, :datom d})))\n   369\t  (let [[e a _v t m] d]\n   370\t    (when-not (and (int? e)\n   371\t                   (keyword? a)\n   372\t                   (some? (namespace a))\n   373\t                   (int? t)\n   374\t                   (int? m))\n   375\t      (throw (ex-info \"Malformed d5 datom in projection input\"\n   376\t                      {:rule :malformed-datom, :datom d})))\n   377\t    (when (= m (:db/retract datom/reserved))\n   378\t      (throw (ex-info \"Retract datom in assert-only projection input\"\n   379\t                      {:rule :retract, :datom d})))\n   380\t    d))\n   381\t\n   382\t\n   383\t(defn frame-datoms\n   384\t  \"§2 root framing: split one datom stream into its rooted graphs. A\n   385\t   :yin/root true marker closes the graph accumulated above it; the next\n   386\t   graph starts empty, so its fact index is a fresh one — emitter\n   387\t   temporary ids may restart at -16 per graph, and never merge across\n   388\t   graphs. Datoms outside the :yin namespace never reach the projection,\n   389\t   so they neither open nor extend a frame: trailing decorations after\n   390\t   the last marker are not a partial graph, and the returned frames carry\n   391\t   :yin/* datoms only. End-of-stream with :yin/* datoms no marker closes\n   392\t   is a diagnostic.\"\n   393\t  [datoms]\n   394\t  (let [[frames partial]\n   395\t        (reduce (fn [[frames frame] d]\n   396\t                  (let [[_e a v] (check-datom d)]\n   397\t                    (cond\n   398\t                      (and (= :yin/root a) (true? v))\n   399\t                      [(conj frames (conj frame d)) []]\n   400\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
