{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'META-PROTOCOL|dimension|canonical' docs/design/datom.md; rg -n 'Architecture B|debruijn|resolved' src/cljc/yin/vm/resolve.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc; ls -la .cpcache target .dart_tool test/cljd-out; command -v java clojure clj bb node dart; cat shadow-cljs.edn\"","aggregated_output":"2:description: Moduli space of tuples; meta-protocol over open dimensions; N-dimensional tuples, entity IDs, content addressing, namespaces; datom defined as [e a v t m]; source scope is interpreter context, never a tuple slot\n7:Tuples are elements in a moduli space, graded by dimension n.\n8:A tuple can be any dimension/size; each dimension dn is a distinct kind of fact, fit for a distinct role.\n9:A datom is the canonical persistent tuple: [e a v t m].\n10:The moduli space is open: applications declare new dimensions as needed. No dimension is canonical.\n20:  d3: (s, a, v). Bare facts (RDF-style triples). The semantic floor for fact-shaped dimensions.\n25:  d1 (content-addressing floor): every tuple of any dimension reduces to its hash, itself a d1 tuple. Universal addressability.\n26:  d3 (semantic floor): every fact-shaped dimension projects to (subject, attribute, value). Universal interpretability for the fact-shaped subset.\n28:Not every dimension is fact-shaped. Some are tuple-shaped without subject (capabilities, signatures, sensor frames).\n31:Universal principles (apply to all dimensions):\n36:  Restrictions are a feature: dimension choice constrains shape, enabling efficient per-shape indexing.\n40:  Each dimension is a different chart on that universe.\n41:  A tuple at dimension n is a tuple-shaped event in n coordinates.\n42:  A datom (the canonical d5 5-tuple [e a v t m]) is like a space-time event:\n45:  Interpreters observe parts of the stream and construct higher-dimensional structures.\n47:  Higher-dimensional tuples can serve specialized streams, for example\n51:# META-PROTOCOL\n53:The meta-protocol is the only universal piece. The vocabulary of dimensions is open.\n55:A dimension dn is declared as a small d3 subgraph:\n58:  :dim/encoding      canonical-encoding rule (parameterized by slot types).\n62:The content hash of this subgraph IS the dimension's identity.\n63:New dimensions are introduced by publishing such a bundle. No external schema authority is needed.\n66:  hash(tuple) = hash(dimension-hash || canonical-encoded-slots-in-order).\n67:  Different dimensions never collide on the same slot values.\n73:  Each new dn publishes morphisms to/from anchor dimensions (typically d3 for fact-shaped, d1 universally).\n77:  Two tuples are equal iff their canonical hashes match within the same dimension.\n78:  Cross-dimensional equality goes through projection: a in dn matches b in dm iff pi(a) = b.\n82:Every slot type has a canonical encoding rule that all implementations must conform to.\n89:  Keywords: namespaced; canonical UTF-8 NFC bytes; namespace and name separated by a fixed delimiter.\n95:  encode(v) = canonical(v)        if |canonical(v)| <= hash-size (32 bytes).\n96:  encode(v) = hash(canonical(v))  otherwise.\n100:A slot value is always small: an inline canonical primitive or a fixed-size content hash.\n104:Every dimension carries content-addressing for free.\n107:  hash(tuple) = hash(dimension-hash || canonical-encoded-slots-in-order).\n109:Cross-dimension addressing:\n115:  Cycles live at the entity layer (in dimensions like d5 where e is a stable local handle): two entities can reference each other via attributes whose v is the other's content hash.\n120:  A stream is identified by the hash of its kickoff metadata (creator, schema, dimension, t-zero), not by its content.\n128:A datom is the canonical 5-tuple shape [e a v t m] like in Datomic, except the m position is a metadata entity ID. It packages fact + transaction + provenance + entity handle + value in one row, suitable for column-store layouts and EAVT/AEVT indexing. It is the dimension most of dao.jing currently uses.\n132:Other dimensions (d1, d3, etc.) are documented separately when introduced.\n158:  v: Value. Inline canonical primitive (<= 32 bytes) or 32-byte content hash.\n189:  v is always small: an inline canonical primitive or a fixed-size content hash.\n301:indexes remain canonical d5.\nrg: src/cljc/yin/vm/resolve.cljc: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:2:  \"D5 of the de Bruijn projection (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:19:   `debruijn/frame-datoms`, before either side persists: a partial frame,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:39:            [yin.vm.debruijn :as debruijn]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:49:  {:yin.debruijn/fingerprint fingerprint,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:50:   :yin.debruijn/datoms (debruijn/projected->datoms {:fingerprint fingerprint,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:76:   (`debruijn/frame-datoms`), or nil when it is exactly one complete\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:82:    (let [frames (debruijn/frame-datoms datoms)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:90:      {:rule (:rule (:diagnostic (debruijn/exception-diagnostic t)))})))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:104:                     (let [p (debruijn/project-datoms datoms)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:107:                       (debruijn/exception-diagnostic t)))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:8:   after it. Labels are symbolic while emitting and are resolved to\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:34:  \"Operand attributes whose emitted value is a label, resolved to the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:234:   ids where a resolved pc belongs, `labels` maps each label to its pc,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:330:   resolved to pcs, no header. `[:call argc tail?]` is kept unfolded for\n.cpcache:\ntotal 1424\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 20 sto  staff     640 Sep 24 00:03 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 48 sto  staff    1536 Sep 23 23:48 ..\n-rw-r--r--@ 