t every-terminal-state-is-idempotent\n1470:(deftest zero-one-and-multi-parameter-lambdas-are-distinct\n1489:(deftest parameter-order-is-identity\n1499:(deftest stream-operations-change-the-fingerprint\n1512:(deftest continuation-markers-are-distinct-and-stable\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md | sed -n '194,243p'; sed -n '1,85p' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc; rg -n 'debruijn/(canonical|encode|dimension|project|numeric)' src/cljc/yin/vm -g '*.cljc'; sed -n '3208,3255p' lib/cljd-out/yin/vm/debruijn.dart; sed -n '1,60p' .dart_tool/package_config.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md | sed -n '194,243p'; sed -n '1,85p' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc; rg -n 'debruijn/(canonical|encode|dimension|project|numeric)' src/cljc/yin/vm -g '*.cljc'; sed -n '3208,3255p' lib/cljd-out/yin/vm/debruijn.dart; sed -n '1,60p' .dart_tool/package_config.json\"","aggregated_output":"   194\tstream's UTF-8 encoding.\n   195\t\n   196\tNumeric canonicalisation (Contract Version 1, `:yin.debruijn/contract-version`\n   197\t1, `:integral-double-folding false`): the classes `:int64` and `:double` are\n   198\tdisjoint. Exact integers in signed int64 use int64 encoding; every\n   199\tfloating-point value, integral or not, uses IEEE-754 double encoding and is\n   200\tnever folded into `:int64`. All NaNs use one quiet-NaN encoding and `+0` and\n   201\t`-0` remain distinct. Consequently integer `1` and double `1.0` project to\n   202\tdistinct records and fingerprints, and `{1 :a, 1.0 :b}` and `#{1 1.0}` do not\n   203\tcollide. An earlier draft folded integral doubles into int64; the\n   204\tunreleased contract never shipped that rule.\n   205\t\n   206\tThe boundary: JavaScript has one number type, so a JS host cannot tell `1`\n   207\tfrom `1.0`. That is a host quirk, and it stays in the JS adapter: on CLJS a\n   208\tsafe integer classifies as `:int64`, a non-integral number as `:double`, and\n   209\tan unsafe integral is diagnostic. It does not contaminate the universal\n   210\tprojection, whose bytes are type-preserving on JVM and Dart. A foreign record\n   211\tclaiming a class the host cannot represent is refused at the boundary with\n   212\t`:unsupported-value`. Bigints, ratios, characters, and other out-of-domain\n   213\tnumeric objects are diagnostic. NFC normalisation collisions are an inherited\n   214\tlimit of the repository encoding.\n   215\tThe Dart NFC source is settled 2026-09-21: the pure-Dart `unorm_dart`\n   216\tpackage (Unicode 16.0) provides NFC on every ClojureDart target. D3 still\n   217\tproves byte identity on all three hosts against the §8 fixtures.\n   218\t\n   219\t## 6. Stream composition and consumers\n   220\t\n   221\tImplementation belongs in `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc`: it consumes the\n   222\texisting `:yin/*` schema and shares no transport code. It exposes:\n   223\t\n   224\t* `project-datoms`: pure projection of one complete rooted graph to Merkle\n   225\t  records and root fingerprint;\n   226\t* `forward-step`: ordinary `dao.stream` forward interpretation. It frames at\n   227\t  the root marker, projects there, emits the projected tuples, and reports a\n   228\t  partial frame at end as diagnostic. Pending writes remain explicit and are\n   229\t  retried only by host cadence.\n   230\t\n   231\tNamed storage, renderers, Datalog queries, source maps, and diagnostics consume\n   232\tthe named stream. Deduplication, cache lookup, and alpha-equivalence consume\n   233\tthe projected segment/fingerprint. The named AST remains the execution source:\n   234\t`yin.vm/ast->datoms` emits named AST datoms; `yin.vm.linearize` lowers those\n   235\tdatoms to `:yin.code/*` datoms, which the semantic VM loads and executes;\n   236\t`yin.vm.ast-walker` can evaluate the named AST map directly. None of these\n   237\texecution paths consumes the de Bruijn projection. A waitset is optional only\n   238\tfor a composition that owns several independent waiters; no lease fact, lease\n   239\ttimer, callback, or scheduler is introduced here.\n   240\t\n   241\t## 7. Implementation plan\n   242\t\n   243\t### D0 — contract, dimension, and fixtures\n(ns yin.vm.debruijn-linearize\n  \"B2 (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md S3.2): the stack lowerer.\n   `lower-stack` consumes `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve/resolve`'s output\n   (resolved tuples plus binder side table) and emits the canonical\n   `:yin.debruijn.code/*` image plus its pc-keyed diagnostic side table,\n   reproducing `yin.vm.linearize/lower`'s own flattening walk itself since\n   `lower` cannot read resolved tuples (it reads `:yin/name` and\n   `:yin/params`, which resolved tuples no longer carry). `adapt` is the\n   composition `lower-stack` after `resolve`. `lift` is the reverse\n   morphism, `:yin.debruijn.code/*` image plus side table back to a named\n   canonical instruction vector; `named-canonical-vector` independently\n   derives that same named vector from `yin.vm.linearize/lower`'s own\n   output, for the structural comparison test and the lift law.\n\n   `yin.vm.linearize` and named lowering semantics are unchanged: this\n   namespace only reads `yin.vm.linearize/lower`'s output (for\n   `named-canonical-vector`) and the public parts of `yin.vm.code`,\n   `yin.vm.debruijn-code`, and `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve`.\"\n  (:require [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn :as debruijn]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-resolve :as resolve]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; named-canonical-vector: lower's :yin.code/* datom batch -> the named\n;; canonical instruction vector (UCF S7.3.2 shape)\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The actual named-path precedent for this step is `yin.vm.semantic/load-\n;; image`'s own local `canonical` step (index the batch by entity, sort\n;; instructions by `:yin.code/pc`, then read each entity's operands off\n;; `yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table` with every `:pc`-kind operand\n;; resolved to a position instead of an entity ref). That helper is\n;; private to a namespace this phase may only read from, so it is\n;; reimplemented here rather than called.\n\n(defn- index-batch\n  \"Entity ids in order of first appearance, and each entity's attribute\n   map. A repeated single-valued attribute keeps its last value, matching\n   every other batch indexer in this lineage.\"\n  [datoms]\n  (reduce (fn [[order attrs] [e a v]]\n            [(if (contains? attrs e) order (conj order e))\n             (assoc-in attrs [e a] v)])\n          [[] {}]\n          datoms))\n\n\n(defn named-canonical-vector\n  \"`lower`'s `:yin.code/*` datom batch turned into `{:vector v :source s}`:\n   `v` is the canonical instruction vector (UCF S7.3.2), pc-indexed,\n   `:yin.code/target`/`:yin.code/body` refs resolved to positions; `s` is\n   the parallel pc-indexed vector of each instruction's `:yin.code/source`\n   AST entity, carried only for diagnostics -- never read by\n   `lower-stack` or `lift` below.\"\n  [datoms]\n  (let [[order attrs] (index-batch datoms)\n        seg (first (filter #(= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type])) order))\n        structural #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op}\n        instructions (filterv (fn [e]\n                                (and (not= e seg)\n                                     (some #(contains? (get attrs e) %) structural)))\n                              order)\n        n (count instructions)\n        by-pc (into {} (map (fn [e] [(get-in attrs [e :yin.code/pc]) e])) instructions)\n        ordered (mapv by-pc (range n))\n        pc-of (zipmap ordered (range))\n        tuple (fn [e]\n                (let [ia (get attrs e)\n                      mnem (:yin.code/op ia)]\n                  (into [mnem]\n                        (map (fn [[a kind]]\n                               (if (= :pc kind)\n                                 (get pc-of (get ia a))\n                                 (get ia a)))\n                             (get code/vector-operand-table mnem)))))]\n    {:vector (mapv tuple ordered)\n     :source (mapv #(get-in attrs [% :yin.code/source]) ordered)}))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; lower-stack: resolved tuples -> :yin.debruijn.code/* image + pc side table\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Reproduces `yin.vm.linearize/flatten-program`'s own recursion (operator\n;; then operands, `if` test then arms, lambda bodies out of line in\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:50:   :yin.debruijn/datoms (debruijn/projected->datoms {:fingerprint fingerprint,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:104:                     (let [p (debruijn/project-datoms datoms)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:25:   distinct from `yin.vm.debruijn/encode-value`: it never applies NFC to\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:98:   `yin.vm.debruijn/canonical-value-table`: :ratio and :bigint are their\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:408:   from `yin.vm.debruijn/encode-value`: exact numeric bits and exact\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:527:   encoder' shape `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash` uses for its\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:541:   for `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash`, so hashing the string directly\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:135:  [[:yin.debruijn/dimension :dim/contract-version contract-version]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:136:   [:yin.debruijn/dimension :dim/arity 22]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:137:   [:yin.debruijn/dimension :dim/slots dimension-slots]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:138:   [:yin.debruijn/dimension\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:147:   [:yin.debruijn/dimension\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:151:   [:yin.debruijn/dimension\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:154:      \"yin.vm.debruijn/project-datoms is the lift\"]]]])\n(fl$62[1]=lcoc_core.PersistentVector(null, 2, 5, arg$41, fl$64, -1, ));\n(fl$61[1]=lcoc_core.PersistentVector(null, 2, 5, arg$42, fl$62, -1, ));\n(fl$61[2]=const lcoc_core.Keyword(null, \"scalars\", 1410720937, ));\n(fl$61[3]=lcoc_core.$_EMPTY_VECTOR);\n(fl$1[21]=lcoc_core.PersistentHashMap(null, lcoc_core.BitmapNode(2, 516, 516, fl$61, ), -1, ));\nreturn lcoc_core.PersistentHashMap(null, lcoc_core.BitmapNode(17, 1767907112, 1751125032, fl$1, ), -1, );\n}();\n\n// END node-grammar\n\n// BEGIN node-hash\ndc.dynamic node_hash(d